 set it somewhere warm to rise for 1-2 hours or until it has doubled in size. The more you bake in your house and the warmer it is, the faster your bread will rise. The extra yeasts in the air and some warmth will do amazing things!","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"zt0oi1431","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-4gvkk","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"rymjm1432","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-dua2i","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"8. When your bread has risen until you can still see your fingerprint when you poke it gently and it doesn't immediately spring back then it's time to form your loaf!","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"cgh4d1434","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-4f4ml","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"rdw441435","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-3b9r7","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"9. Roll it back out onto a floured board and degassed the dough by folding it over on itself 3-4 times, then started rolling it up like a newspaper fairly tightly. You want to get some decent surface tension going on the outside of the loaf you are forming.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"8v66r1437","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-68dh8","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"u2kmg1438","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-6kb44","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"10. I put it in a floured proofing basket called a banneton to do its second rise. Cover it with the cover that often comes with your banneton or very loosely with plastic wrap that you've sprayed with oil.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"tx08m1440","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-9jo6d","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"k1ec81441","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-47q0d","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Tip - You can make a proofing basket out of a bread basket or pan lined with towels but it's not nearly as convenient if you make bread regularly.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"4sty51443","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-39ugd","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"sn0s81444","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-eerg5","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"11. Put a cast iron dutch oven in your oven and begin heating it to 450 degrees for at least 30 minutes.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"aznzl1446","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-81n3s","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"9jgfe1447","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-bp0jp","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"12. After 30 minutes, very carefully remove the preheated dutch oven to your stove top leaving the lid on.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"q9uk91449","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-8gfu","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"ml9a21450","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-obar","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"13. Roll your loaf out of the basket and onto a piece of super handy parchment paper sprinkled with polenta or semolina flour and score as shown above or in the pattern of your choice.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"nkss01452","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-6ggj4","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"j6vy51453","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-cds65","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"14. Remove the lid from the dutch oven with an oven mitt and gently transfer your loaf by lifting the parchment paper and setting the whole thing in the pot paper and all. Replace the lid using an oven mitt and put the whole dutch oven back into your oven.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"dsikc1455","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-18uuj","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"uqmfq1456","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-difv2","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"15. Turn down the temperature on the oven to 425 at this point and bake for 30 minutes. If you would like a darker crust you may want to keep it on a higher temperature but it should be done within 40 minutes. Bread is done when it sounds hollow when tapped on the bottom.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"a9z8y1458","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-40ikf","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"mjlj91459","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-b082s","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Please let this cool at least an hour before slicing. It will make all the difference in the texture of this bread. If placed in a plastic bag after completely cooled the crust will soften and become more chewy.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"}],"metadata":{"version":1.0,"createdTimestamp":"2023-02-18T22:13:17.092Z","updatedTimestamp":"2023-02-18T22:13:17.092Z","id":"f835037e-94ab-496d-a960-a046be8fa9ad"},"documentStyle":{}},"instructions":"1. Add the flours, butter, salt and yeast to a stand mixer ( I don't sift) and mix on low speed with the hook attachment to somewhat combine.\n\n \n\n2. Add the molasses, honey and sourdough starter all at once and mix again on low speed to somewhat combine.\n\n \n\n3. Begin to drizzle in the liquid very slowly with the machine running on low speed until you have used a little more than 1 cup.\n\n \n\n4. Allow the machine to continue to work the dough until it begins to pull away from the sides of the mixer adding more liquid tablespoon by tablespoon if it seems too dry. If you go too fast and the dough gets too wet add more bread flour a few tablespoons at a time. The dough should become just slightly sticky and be very soft in about 5 minutes. Let the machine knead a few minutes after it comes together maybe 7-8 minutes total. The dough should be just tacky now and still very soft.\n\n \n\n5. Turn the dough out onto a floured board with a spatula. Flour your hands and then knead the dough just a few times by folding it over itself one direction and then the other.\n\n \n\n6. Gather the dough into a ball and place it in a large bowl that has a tablespoon of oil in it and roll it around so it's lightly covered with the oil.\n\n \n\n7. Cover the bowl with plastic wrap and set it somewhere warm to rise for 1-2 hours or until it has doubled in size. The more you bake in your house and the warmer it is, the faster your bread will rise. The extra yeasts in the air and some warmth will do amazing things!\n\n \n\n8. When your bread has risen until you can still see your fingerprint when you poke it gently and it doesn't immediately spring back then it's time to form your loaf!\n\n \n\n 9. Roll it back out onto a floured board and degassed the dough by folding it over on itself 3-4 times, then started rolling it up like a newspaper fairly tightly. You want to get some decent surface tension going on the outside of the loaf you are forming.\n\n \n\n10. I put it in a floured proofing basket called a banneton to do its second rise. Cover it with the cover that often comes with your banneton or very loosely with plastic wrap that you've sprayed with oil.\n\n \n\nTip - You can make a proofing basket out of a bread basket or pan lined with towels but it's not nearly as convenient if you make bread regularly.\n\n \n\n11. Put a cast iron dutch oven in your oven and begin heating it to 450 degrees for at least 30 minutes. \n\n \n\n12. After 30 minutes, very carefully remove the preheated dutch oven to your stove top leaving the lid on.\n\n \n\n13. Roll your loaf out of the basket and onto a piece of super handy parchment paper sprinkled with polenta or semolina flour and score as shown above or in the pattern of your choice.\n\n \n\n14. Remove the lid from the dutch oven with an oven mitt and gently transfer your loaf by lifting the parchment paper and setting the whole thing in the pot paper and all. Replace the lid using an oven mitt and put the whole dutch oven back into your oven. \n\n \n\n15. Turn down the temperature on the oven to 425 at this point and bake for 30 minutes. If you would like a darker crust you may want to keep it on a higher temperature but it should be done within 40 minutes. Bread is done when it sounds hollow when tapped on the bottom.\n\n \n\nPlease let this cool at least an hour before slicing. It will make all the difference in the texture of this bread. If placed in a plastic bag after completely cooled the crust will soften and become more chewy.","title":"Hazelnut Molasses Sourdough Bread"}],"dynamicUrl":"/bread-and-sourdough/{title}","config":{"dataset":{"lowercase":true,"collection":"Recipes11","pageSize":1.0,"filter":{"link-recipes-1-title":"/bread-and-sourdough/hazelnut-molasses-sourdough-bread"},"collectionName":"Recipes11","sort":[{"order":"asc"}],"seoV2":true}},"totalCount":1.0,"schemas":{"Recipes11":{"displayName":"Bread & Sourdough Recipes","plugins":{},"allowedOperations":["patch","isReferenced","insert","save","bulkInsert","bulkUpdate","update","truncate","remove","removeReference","count","bulkPatch","find","replaceReferences","bulkRemove","insertReference","get","bulkSave","queryReferenced","distinct","aggregate"],"id":"Recipes11","fields":{"introduction":{"displayName":"Introduction ","sortable":true,"isDeleted":false,"type":"text","index":9.0},"servings":{"displayName":"servings","sortable":true,"isDeleted":false,"type":"text","index":1.0},"photo":{"displayName":"Photo","sortable":true,"isDeleted":false,"type":"image","index":19.0},"link-recipes-1-title":{"displayName":"Bread & Sourdough1 (Name)","calculator":{"config":{"lowercase":true,"pattern":"/bread-and-sourdough/{title}"}},"sortable":false,"queryOperators":["eq","ne","lt","gt","lte","gte","exists"],"isDeleted":false,"type":"pagelink","index":15.0},"url":{"displayName":"URL","sortable":true,"isDeleted":false,"type":"url","index":18.0},"image":{"displayName":"Image","sortable":true,"isDeleted":false,"type":"image","index":2.0},"_id":{"displayName":"ID","sortable":true,"isDelet